<h1>Navigation</h1>

<h2><code>PeriodStepper</code></h2>
<p>A previous arrow, the period's label and a next arrow in one row: <em>16 – 22 Nov</em>, <em>November 2026</em> or <em>2026</em>. The arrows are round buttons at <code>--touch-target</code>, a left chevron for previous and a right chevron for next, named by <code>Kind</code> (<code>Week</code>, <code>Month</code> or <code>Year</code>): "Previous week", "Next month". An arrow is disabled, at <code>--opacity-disabled</code>, when its flag is false: <code>CanGoPrevious</code> at the first period with data, <code>CanGoNext</code> at the period that holds today. The label is a polite live region, so a screen reader hears the new period; a long label wraps.</p>
<ul>
    <li><code>OnPrevious</code> and <code>OnNext</code> are raised by the arrows.</li>
    <li><code>data-testid</code>: <code>TestId</code>, and as a prefix: <code>{TestId}-previous</code>, <code>{TestId}-label</code>, <code>{TestId}-next</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2><code>BackHandler</code></h2>
<p>Renders nothing. While <code>OnBack</code> is set, the Android back button runs it instead of leaving the app; the newest handler on screen wins, and removing the component or clearing <code>OnBack</code> unregisters it. A failure in <code>OnBack</code> goes to the nearest error boundary, as one from a button click would. It needs the <code>IBackButton</code> service from <code>AddShieldsUi()</code>; the host calls <code>IBackButton.HandleAsync()</code>, on the instance from the WebView's scope, when the system back is pressed.</p>

